#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124370 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124674 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "53776591-13cc-549e-9f32-c5abaeced1dc" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Growth"}] :question/id #uuid "a568fc63-e81c-5210-93f0-bcbd429a9e40" :question/math-academy-id "e-6678" :question/problem "A biologist observed that the growth of the number of fish in a lake is directly proportional to the difference between the lake’s maximum fish population and the current population. Let $P(t)$ be the fish population, measured in number of fish, and let $t \\ge 0$ be the time in years. If the maximum fish population that the lake can support is $200$ fish, which differential equation could be used to model the lake’s fish population? Assume that $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the fish population $P(t)$ of the lake changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum fish population the lake can support and the current fish population. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d P}{\\textrm d t} = k(a - P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum fish population the lake can support, and $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a = 200 \\textrm{ fish},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d P}{\\textrm d t} = k(200 - P).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124371 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124675 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b793cda9-e11f-5a48-8053-bb75991c4c42" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Growth Model"}] :question/id #uuid "d603efdd-6e27-5e14-a106-f9fc4a182403" :question/math-academy-id "e-6679" :question/problem "In an aquarium, there are initially $20$ fish. After $30$ weeks, the number of fish is $50.$ Assuming inhibited growth, calculate the number of fish after $100$ weeks if the maximum capacity of the aquarium is $90$ fish." :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the number of fish $N(t)$ changes is proportional to the difference between the total number of fish and the current number of fish. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}N}{\\mathrm{d}t}=k\\left(a-N\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum capacity of the aquarium, and $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a = 90$ fish, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d} N}{\\mathrm{d} t} = k(90-N).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dN}{90-N} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dN}{90-N} & =\\int k\\,dt \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|90-N| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $90 - N > 0$ for all $t \\ge 0.$\nNext, we solve for $N(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}90-N & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 90-N & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 90-N & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ N & =90-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=e^{-C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial number of fish was $20.$ So, we apply the initial condition $N(0)=20,$ and solve for $K{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =20 \\\\ 90-K{e}^{0} & =20 \\\\ 90-K & =20 \\\\ K & =70\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $N(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nN(t)=90 - 70 e^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $30$ weeks, the number of fish is $50.$ So, applying the condition $N(30)=50,$ we can solve for $k{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(30) & =50 \\\\ 90-70{e}^{-30k} & =50 \\\\ 40 & =70{e}^{-30k} \\\\ {e}^{-30k} & =\\frac{4}{7} \\\\ -30k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{7}) \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{30}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{7})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nN(t)=90-70e^{(t/30)\\,\\ln\\left(4/7\\right)}.\n$$\n\nFinally,  the number of fish after $t=100$ weeks is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(100) & =90-70{e}^{(100/30)\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4/7)} \\\\  & =79.161… \\\\  & ≈79\\,\\text{fish},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124373 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124673 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ade69467-c229-509d-9bdc-b28935b2d1d8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Inhibited Growth Equations and Initial Value Problems"}] :question/id #uuid "d178c83a-b8af-5d6a-bf40-fcfb7506feeb" :question/math-academy-id "e-6686" :question/problem "Given that $y \\lt 60$ and $t \\ge 0,$ which of the following is an inhibited growth equation?\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 240 - 4y$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 4y - 240$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 4y^2 - 240$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 4 - 240y$" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = k(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > y$ for all $t \\ge 0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 240 - 4y.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 4(60-y).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=4,$ and $a=60.$ Note that for inhibited growth, we must have $y \\lt 60$ for all $t \\ge 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124762 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124673 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ade69467-c229-509d-9bdc-b28935b2d1d8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Inhibited Growth Equations and Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f22971a2-d45d-52ec-99fc-a349d577bfb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} =-0.5(y + 150)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121170} {:answer/id #uuid "2435e95e-56ae-5f52-9016-f3f3c23c3ab6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 150(y + 0.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121171} {:answer/id #uuid "36f7d395-bab4-516e-8f87-8da116b30f32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 150(y - 0.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121172} {:answer/id #uuid "632fa9b9-0103-5768-84d1-c9f8cc114907" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 0.5(150 - y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121173} {:answer/id #uuid "64de0030-8615-5d24-93b0-9b93a8fa45fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 0.5(y - 150)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121174}] :answer-field/id #uuid "79311994-ead5-5028-b4a4-de97196cfb35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121169}] :question/id #uuid "3ff73a32-c10e-5c6a-a056-14d519235f78" :question/math-academy-id "q-106661" :question/problem "Given that $y < 150$ and $t \\ge 0$, which of the following is an inhibited growth equation?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124763 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124673 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ade69467-c229-509d-9bdc-b28935b2d1d8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Inhibited Growth Equations and Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd57bce4-bf4d-5488-ada6-93439471fb78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 5y^{2} - 400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121176} {:answer/id #uuid "1f00b80a-9440-52ad-bb3e-9a6954010114" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 5 - 400y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121177} {:answer/id #uuid "34a003e9-7090-500a-bcd5-5a07bde1c2a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 400 - 5y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121178} {:answer/id #uuid "35a91da3-7b8e-54e8-8314-4340d804eddd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 5y - 400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121179} {:answer/id #uuid "6623a9f2-ffe7-561f-9984-2a59cbb33aa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 400 - 5y^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121180}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b4164cc7-8b43-5b53-b800-89f7a6270478" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121175} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd4d9520-8eb8-590a-8484-786df4886f48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=5-400y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397851} {:answer/id #uuid "4b2d8367-f11c-56cb-b66f-27a8c632bc41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=5y-400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397852} {:answer/id #uuid "906da5cb-d9e8-5ec3-b4fd-cd8abe7cdb40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=400-5y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397853} {:answer/id #uuid "fa1db174-5614-5103-8d7e-cb38f5042fa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=5{y}^{2}-400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397854} {:answer/id #uuid "a5c36cb8-43dc-59ec-b220-e1d3bce29295" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=400-5{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397855}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "906da5cb-d9e8-5ec3-b4fd-cd8abe7cdb40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=400-5y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397853} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b3476a6-1afc-5395-a095-cd169e19c687" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397850}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "092a8c01-7fc1-532c-ba20-9cb4c823a717" :question/math-academy-id "q-106662" :question/problem "Given that $y<80$ and $t\\ge 0,$ which of the following is an inhibited growth equation?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=k(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=400-5y.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=5(80-y).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=5,$ and $a=80.$ Note that for inhibited growth, we must have $y<80$ for all $t\\ge 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124766 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124674 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "53776591-13cc-549e-9f32-c5abaeced1dc" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Growth"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a081d98d-bac7-5472-ac1f-bb81d8502479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt} =-k(70 - P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121194} {:answer/id #uuid "aab37068-8f4f-5403-92f7-cb76580142a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt} = kP(70 - P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121195} {:answer/id #uuid "d2c5837d-8d9b-5bdc-a3d7-21a16439da00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt} = kP^{2}(70 - P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121196} {:answer/id #uuid "d5d3c042-9051-5258-8e61-7f0076523ae3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt} = k(70 - P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121197} {:answer/id #uuid "1efedcd4-3e2c-5390-917a-75608a7b4a6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt} =-k(70 + P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121198}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d06da1af-5ad6-5a97-9279-9b6036bd2a5d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121193}] :question/id #uuid "19a4e47b-fcd5-50aa-804a-b275eeea23d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-106691" :question/problem "A factory's management noted that the growth of the unit production is directly proportional to the difference between the maximum number of units a factory worker can produce and the current production quantity. Let $P(t)$ be the production quantity, measured in units produced, and let $t \\ge 0$ be the time in days. If the maximum number of units that a worker can produce is $70$ $units/day$, which differential equation could be used to model the factory's production? Assume that $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308739 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124674 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "53776591-13cc-549e-9f32-c5abaeced1dc" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Growth"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04aedd39-6cd0-5b6c-a95b-8f1528463bef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(95-T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305194} {:answer/id #uuid "100e6d8a-679a-59a7-9f49-904c4ba960dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(30-T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305298} {:answer/id #uuid "3d8045ca-7194-5335-8e8c-fb093e38eb2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(60-T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305744} {:answer/id #uuid "7c223239-ad05-5364-8640-ceef9053e8b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(100-T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306336} {:answer/id #uuid "eb832421-670f-5c24-a2cd-1bebc2d73fe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(950-T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307318}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04aedd39-6cd0-5b6c-a95b-8f1528463bef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(95-T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305194} :answer-field/id #uuid "69e66b97-0745-5e34-a590-bf57a3e5e855" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307862}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5651275c-fd64-5799-85e0-52ab15ebd288" :question/math-academy-id "q-335142" :question/problem "A tutoring center currently has $10$ enrolled students. The number of students in the center $T(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in months. If the maximum capacity of the tutoring center is $95$ students, which differential equation could be used to model the student population of the center? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Since the number of students in the center obeys an inhibited growth model, the rate at which the number of students changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum capacity and the number of students in the center. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-T),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum capacity of the tutoring center, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=95$ students, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=k(95-T).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402133 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124675 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b793cda9-e11f-5a48-8053-bb75991c4c42" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Growth Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "088dc229-13bb-525f-97f0-615a3387e9fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$35$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402135} {:answer/id #uuid "dbe81525-8780-55b2-9e79-394a40fd9a7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$50$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402136} {:answer/id #uuid "0d9dd8e3-5764-58b2-9ff2-4b88c32898f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$40$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402137} {:answer/id #uuid "ac5c6a61-12a4-5352-8fcc-e3319bbc18bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$20$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402138} {:answer/id #uuid "dbb667f2-6fca-5729-a7fd-9dfe18862602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$28$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402139}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "088dc229-13bb-525f-97f0-615a3387e9fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$35$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402135} :answer-field/id #uuid "63531a29-5d3e-5f5f-97ce-ef01b7d03202" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402134}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a4fd0915-73cc-575a-b3cc-4a156a96dd49" :question/math-academy-id "q-335542" :question/problem "A wildlife reserve can support a total of $800$ deer.\nAt the beginning of a disease study in the reserve, a parasitic infection has affected $80$ deer. After $10$ days, the number of deer affected by the parasite increases to $300.$ To the nearest day, how long does it take for the infected deer population to reach $600$ animals?\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the number of deer affected by the parasite, $P(t),$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time, in days.*" :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the deer population affected by the parasite, $P(t),$ changes is proportional to the difference between the total deer population and its current population affected. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the total deer population in the reserve, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=800$ deer, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(800-P).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{800-P} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dP}{800-P} & =\\int k\\,dt \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|800-P| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $800-P>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\nNext, we solve for $P(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}800-P & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 800-P & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 800-P & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ P & =800-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial deer population affected by the parasite was $80$ animals. So, we apply the initial condition $P(0)=80,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =80 \\\\ 800-K{e}^{0} & =80 \\\\ 800-K & =80 \\\\ K & =720\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $P(t):$\n\n$$\nP(t)=800-720{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $10$ days, the deer population affected by the parasite was $300$ animals. So, applying the condition $P(10)=300,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(10) & =300 \\\\ 800-720{e}^{-10k} & =300 \\\\ 500 & =720{e}^{-10k} \\\\ {e}^{-10k} & =\\frac{500}{720}=\\frac{25}{36} \\\\ -10k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{25}{36}) \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{25}{36})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when $600$ deer are affected by the parasite. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({t}_{1}) & =600 \\\\ 800-720{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =600 \\\\ 200 & =720{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{200}{720}=\\frac{5}{18} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{18}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{18}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that $600$ deer will be affected by the parasite after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{18})}{-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{25}{36})}=35.128…≈35\\,\\text{days},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest day."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317472 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124674 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "53776591-13cc-549e-9f32-c5abaeced1dc" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Growth"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17d8e18d-55da-5747-a381-b32f0cd64912" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=-k(100-F)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315254} {:answer/id #uuid "2663e8c9-ddca-5ed4-a0c7-4cf541120ddf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=k{F}^{2}(100-F)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315335} {:answer/id #uuid "5c1c60c4-ae35-5c6d-b92a-7fc0de21cd3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=kF(100-F)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315644} {:answer/id #uuid "653fb905-e4dc-5614-96c4-2effafd1ebcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=k(100-F)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315681} {:answer/id #uuid "a062595a-6ef4-5175-b64d-a91182de7ac5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=-k(100+F)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316021}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "653fb905-e4dc-5614-96c4-2effafd1ebcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=k(100-F)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315681} :answer-field/id #uuid "360d624d-1168-5448-a7ea-9e7a2bd530a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a2bd5b32-0a6c-5631-90f3-4c8beb909e62" :question/math-academy-id "q-344214" :question/problem "An ecologist found that the growth of the number of frogs in a wetland is directly proportional to the difference between the wetland’s maximum frog population and the current population. Let $F(t)$ be the frog population, measured in number of frogs, and let $t\\ge 0$ be the time in years. If the maximum frog population the wetland can support is $100$ frogs, which differential equation could be used to model the frog population? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the frog population $F(t)$ of the wetland changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum frog population the wetland can support and the current frog population. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-F),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum frog population the wetland can support, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=100\\text{ frogs},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=k(100-F).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125068 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124675 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b793cda9-e11f-5a48-8053-bb75991c4c42" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Growth Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89a1b567-4d92-50bc-9b67-c3e259e0a3ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$468$ eggs" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123009} {:answer/id #uuid "e5843acc-3b0b-5b91-aef2-abf1ba9f4627" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$489$ eggs" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123010} {:answer/id #uuid "1f3ffd56-67f0-5967-8b78-0029b0163067" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$420$ eggs" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123011} {:answer/id #uuid "2e5bb5bc-7e52-5d0e-a372-536b69460728" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$486$ eggs" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123012} {:answer/id #uuid "690e1fd9-4928-5f3a-bdee-05eccf3128d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$442$ eggs" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123013}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ce0d7ef1-07b3-513a-a823-292131ee0d53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123008}] :question/id #uuid "3ce256b3-4ef6-51c4-ab72-48603b95ab11" :question/math-academy-id "q-37389" :question/problem "A poultry farmer has egg-laying chickens. She knows that her farm can produce a maximum of $500$ eggs a day; however, it is far behind it.\nSince she started monitoring, the chickens laid $390$ eggs and $400$ eggs on days $3$ and $6$, respectively. How many eggs do the chickens lay on day $42$?\nRound your answer to the nearest integer.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the egg production $E(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t$ is the time, in days.*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125069 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124675 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b793cda9-e11f-5a48-8053-bb75991c4c42" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Growth Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0160e30a-5846-5aa6-86ef-a585cedd44ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$986$ students" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321161} {:answer/id #uuid "71929081-a7d5-5326-a8f5-4740571cce74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$905$ students" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322037} {:answer/id #uuid "78c60709-1e3a-58f3-8b53-ecf0dc4c70cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1005$ students" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322096} {:answer/id #uuid "cd55f1ff-b05d-5c57-bf3a-581ad4b6e03b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$942$ students" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322746} {:answer/id #uuid "ddf858e4-c93a-5010-9a90-68721ca8ff55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$850$ students" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322881}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71929081-a7d5-5326-a8f5-4740571cce74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$905$ students" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322037} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6bfea20-78cb-5933-b3c2-3d3bdf908386" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323559}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7d1a929a-56b1-5075-bd38-17c32dd77741" :question/math-academy-id "q-37400" :question/problem "The population of students $P(t)$ in a school can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.016(2\\,500-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in years. If the initial population of the school is $800$ students, calculate the population after $4$ years. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We're told that the population in the school is modeled by the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.016(2\\,500-P).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{2\\,500-P} & =0.016\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dP}{2\\,500-P} & =\\int 0.016\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2\\,500-P| & =0.016t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $2\\,500-P>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\nNext, we solve for $P(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2500-P & ={e}^{-0.016t-C} \\\\ 2500-P & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-0.016t} \\\\ 2,500-P & =K{e}^{-0.016t} \\\\ P & =2\\,500-K{e}^{-0.016t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the initial population of the school was $800$ students. So, we apply the initial condition $P(0)=800,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}800 & =2\\,500-K{e}^{0} \\\\ 800 & =2\\,500-K \\\\ K & =1\\,700\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nP(t)=2\\,500-1\\,700{e}^{-0.016t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the population in the school after $t=4$ years is\n\n$$\nP(4)=2\\,500-1\\,700{e}^{-0.016\\cdot 4}≈905\\,\\text{students},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}]}
